VERDICT -- mcand (phase 1, equal work)
======================================
BRANCHING'S SIGN FLIPS WITH INSTANCE SHAPE, AND THE SHIPPED DEFAULT IGNORES SHAPE.

All cells OGC_WORKCAP=5000, WORKERS=1 -- deterministic, one draw per cell is exact.

                        M=1          M=2      M=3      M=4      M=6      M=8
  P1  150 blk / 3 bay      492,458   +42.6%   +56.0%   +80.8%   +73.1%   +54.8%
  P3  200 blk / 4 bay    4,367,826   + 4.7%   +16.0%   +11.0%   + 8.8%   +15.1%
  P16 300 blk / 5 bay    3,097,236   + 1.3%   +13.7%   +27.0%   +41.2%   +57.6%
  P13 300 blk / 2 bay   88,534,512   + 0.9%   + 0.1%   - 0.5%   - 3.7%   - 9.5%

P13 is the only instance where opening the permutation dimension pays, and there it pays
monotonically to -9.5% at M=8.  It is also the only one with 300 blocks in 2 bays -- peak_util 4.09
against 1.02 / 1.18 / 1.23 for the other three.  Where the yard is that crowded, WHICH block goes
next decides the layout; where it is not, depth decides it and branching just costs levels.

THE SHIPPED DEFAULT IS OGC_MSET="1,2" INDEXED BY wid%2.  That is a portfolio axis: one worker in
three runs M=2 regardless of the instance.  The measurements say M is not a portfolio question --
M=2 costs prob_1 42.6% and M=8 buys prob_13 9.5%, so a constant is wrong in both directions at once.

AND UNLIKE THE NINE GATES THAT FAILED TODAY, A SEPARATING FEATURE ALREADY EXISTS.  peak_util puts
the branching-friendly instance at 4.09 and the branching-hostile ones at 1.02-1.23, with nothing in
between; blocks-per-bay says the same (150 at 4.09 vs 30-60).  The gap is an order of magnitude
wider than the P33/P7 boundary that was rejected earlier today.

WHAT THIS CANNOT DECIDE YET.  Equal work is the right control for "does branching search better per
unit of compute", not for what to ship.  The competition budget is wall clock, and `work +=
nbeam*M` means a larger M spends its budget faster -- fewer beam draws per run.  ogc_fast.cpp
prescribes the two-part evaluation and this is part one; part two is throughput at wall clock, and
quality-at-work = throughput x budget is what a shipping value has to be chosen on.

METHOD NOTE.  OGC_WORKCAP made every cell here exact -- three verification runs of prob_1 returned
568,924 to the digit.  Every wall-clock experiment earlier today fought 17-35% run-to-run spread
while chasing 5% effects, and two of them (probe, gainfair-K) were void for unrelated reasons.  This
sweep cost 24 cells and settled a question that would have needed hundreds of noisy ones.
